# Error Handling Best Practices

## Error Boundaries

- Implement error boundaries for component trees
- Use error.tsx files in Next.js App Router
- Create reusable error boundary components
- Show user-friendly error messages
- Provide error recovery actions
- Log errors for monitoring
- Reset error state when appropriate
- Handle different error types differently

## Try-Catch Blocks

- Use try-catch for async operations
- Wrap Server Actions in try-catch
- Handle specific error types
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Log errors appropriately
- Don't swallow errors silently
- Clean up resources in finally blocks
- Return consistent error formats

## Server Action Errors

- Always wrap Server Actions in try-catch
- Validate input before processing
- Return consistent error responses
- Handle Supabase errors specifically
- Don't expose internal error details
- Log errors for debugging
- Return user-friendly messages
- Include error codes when helpful

## API Route Errors

- Return proper HTTP status codes
- Use NextResponse for error responses
- Provide clear error messages
- Don't expose stack traces
- Log errors server-side
- Handle validation errors separately
- Return consistent error structure
- Include request IDs for tracking

## Supabase Errors

- Handle Supabase error codes specifically
- Check for auth errors (401, 403)
- Handle network errors gracefully
- Parse Supabase error messages
- Provide user-friendly alternatives
- Log Supabase errors appropriately
- Implement retry logic for transient failures
- Handle rate limiting errors

## Form Validation Errors

- Show field-level error messages
- Display errors near relevant fields
- Use clear, actionable messages
- Show all errors or one at a time (consistent)
- Clear errors appropriately
- Prevent submission with errors
- Use ARIA attributes for accessibility
- Style errors clearly

## Network Errors

- Handle offline scenarios
- Implement retry logic
- Show connection status
- Provide offline functionality when possible
- Cache data for offline access
- Detect connectivity changes
- Show appropriate loading states
- Implement timeout handling

## Loading States

- Show loading indicators for async operations
- Implement skeleton screens
- Use Suspense boundaries
- Show progress for long operations
- Disable actions during loading
- Provide loading feedback immediately
- Handle loading state cleanup
- Consider optimistic updates

## User Feedback

- Show success messages after operations
- Use toast notifications appropriately
- Keep messages concise and clear
- Provide actionable next steps
- Use appropriate notification levels (error, warning, info, success)
- Implement dismissible notifications
- Consider notification position and timing
- Test with screen readers

## Error Logging

- Log errors to monitoring service (Sentry, LogRocket)
- Include relevant context (user ID, action, timestamp)
- Don't log sensitive information
- Use structured logging
- Implement error severity levels
- Include stack traces in logs
- Log user actions before errors
- Set up alerts for critical errors

## Validation Errors

- Validate on client and server
- Use Zod for schema validation
- Show clear validation messages
- Validate early (on blur or submit)
- Provide format examples
- Highlight invalid fields
- Allow easy error correction
- Test validation edge cases

## Authentication Errors

- Handle expired sessions gracefully
- Redirect to login when appropriate
- Show clear auth error messages
- Implement proper logout on auth errors
- Handle token refresh failures
- Provide re-authentication flow
- Don't expose auth internals
- Log authentication failures

## Database Errors

- Handle unique constraint violations
- Handle foreign key violations
- Handle connection errors
- Provide user-friendly messages
- Don't expose database details
- Log database errors
- Implement retry for transient errors
- Handle timeouts appropriately

## Error Recovery

- Provide retry buttons
- Implement automatic retry for transient errors
- Clear error state on retry
- Redirect to safe state when needed
- Save user input before retry
- Provide alternative actions
- Handle partial failures
- Test recovery flows

## Error Messages

- Use clear, non-technical language
- Explain what happened
- Suggest how to fix it
- Avoid blame ("You entered...", use "Please enter...")
- Be specific about the problem
- Provide relevant context
- Keep messages concise
- Test messages with users

## Development vs Production

- Show detailed errors in development
- Show generic errors in production
- Use different error components per environment
- Log differently per environment
- Test production error handling
- Monitor production errors
- Don't expose sensitive info in production
- Have fallback error handling

## 404 and Not Found

- Create custom 404 pages (not-found.tsx)
- Provide helpful navigation
- Suggest related content
- Include search functionality
- Log 404 errors
- Redirect common typos
- Maintain branding in error pages
- Test 404 scenarios

## Rate Limiting Errors

- Handle rate limit responses (429)
- Show retry-after information
- Implement exponential backoff
- Cache responses when possible
- Show user-friendly rate limit messages
- Log rate limit hits
- Implement client-side throttling
- Consider alternative actions

## Error Testing

- Test error scenarios
- Test error boundaries
- Test network failures
- Test validation errors
- Test edge cases
- Test error recovery
- Use error state in Storybook
- Conduct user testing with errors

## Best Practices

- Handle errors at appropriate levels
- Provide context with errors
- Log errors consistently
- Show user-friendly messages
- Implement error monitoring
- Test error scenarios thoroughly
- Document error handling patterns
- Review error logs regularly
- Continuously improve error handling
- Consider error analytics
